% ====================== Abstract ======================
\begin{abstract}
\noindent We present an end-to-end multilingual text-to-image retrieval
system that accepts queries through three modalities --- typed text, spoken
voice, and a reference image --- and retrieves visually relevant photographs
from a 5{,}000-image MS-COCO gallery using a fine-tuned multilingual CLIP
encoder. The system pairs a fine-tuned \texttt{M-CLIP/XLM-RoBERTa-Large-Vit-B-32}
backbone with an OpenAI Whisper-small speech encoder and a FAISS flat-IP
similarity index, deployed behind a FastAPI server and a React/TypeScript
single-page frontend. Beyond engineering, we report a controlled
pretrained-vs-fine-tuned comparison across six languages
(\textit{en, ar, zh, fr, de, ru}) on 15{,}012 queries, observing that scaling
training data from 1{,}000 to 5{,}000 images does not lift overall Recall@1
and we propose a capacity-bottleneck hypothesis to explain this plateau.
Manual testing in Azerbaijani --- a language outside our fine-tuning set ---
exposes a \emph{cross-lingual homograph collision} in mCLIP's shared
embedding space, which we document, characterise, and mitigate in the
user-facing application through a heuristic warning. The complete system,
including reproducible scripts, evaluation reports, and frontend, is
publicly available at
\url{https://github.com/Bayrii/text2image-multilingual-retrieval}.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} multilingual CLIP, cross-modal retrieval, FAISS,
contrastive learning, speech retrieval, low-resource languages, homograph
collision.

\vspace{0.5em}

% ====================== 1. Introduction ======================
\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
Visual search is now embedded in mainstream consumer products: Google Lens,
Pinterest Lens, Amazon visual search, and e-commerce ``shop the look''
features all let users find images by describing them, photographing them,
or pointing a camera at a scene. The overwhelming majority of these systems
are trained on English-dominated data and degrade sharply when the user
issues a query in a low-resource language. This biases the experience
against the very users for whom textual search is hardest to type --- often
the speakers of scripts that lack ergonomic keyboards (Arabic, Cyrillic,
ideographic) or users with low literacy in any single language.

This project asks a tractable version of the question: \emph{can a single
multilingual CLIP-based retrieval system, fine-tuned on a small bilingually
balanced corpus and deployed on consumer hardware, deliver acceptable
retrieval quality across high- and low-resource languages, and can it be
made accessible through voice and image modalities so that text fluency is
not a prerequisite?}

\subsection{Contributions}
The contributions of this work are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
  \item A reproducible pipeline (data collection $\to$ MT-based caption
    augmentation $\to$ image-id-level split $\to$ fine-tuning $\to$
    FAISS index build $\to$ evaluation $\to$ serving), runnable end-to-end
    on a single 6\,GB consumer GPU.
  \item A three-modality user interface --- text, voice, image --- exposed
    through a FastAPI backend and a React/TypeScript frontend, with the
    voice path implemented through OpenAI Whisper-small running on CPU.
  \item A controlled empirical comparison of pretrained vs.\ fine-tuned
    mCLIP on identical 15{,}012-query test sets across six languages, with
    per-language Recall@1, Recall@5, and mAP@10.
  \item A documented \emph{negative result}: scaling training data
    fivefold from 1\,K to 5\,K images does not improve overall Recall@1, and
    we propose a trainable-capacity bottleneck explanation.
  \item A documented qualitative finding: cross-lingual homograph collisions
    in mCLIP's shared embedding space, observed during Azerbaijani query
    testing, together with a heuristic mitigation at the UI layer.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Outline}
Section~\ref{sec:related} situates the project within prior work on
multilingual CLIP and cross-lingual retrieval. Section~\ref{sec:arch}
describes the system architecture. Section~\ref{sec:training} details
the fine-tuning methodology. Section~\ref{sec:impl} covers implementation.
Section~\ref{sec:results} presents the quantitative comparison.
Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses the homograph finding and the
training-data plateau. Section~\ref{sec:future} outlines future work.

% ====================== 2. Related Work ======================
\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\textbf{CLIP and multilingual CLIP.}
Radford et al.~\cite{clip} introduced CLIP, a dual-encoder vision--language
model trained on 400M image--text pairs with a contrastive InfoNCE
objective. mCLIP~\cite{mclip} replaces the original BPE text encoder with
\texttt{XLM-RoBERTa-Large}~\cite{xlmr} and re-aligns it to the frozen CLIP
visual encoder using a multilingual sentence-level alignment loss. The
resulting model embeds images and text in any of XLM-R's 100 pretrained
languages into a shared 512-dimensional space. We use the
\texttt{M-CLIP/XLM-Roberta-Large-Vit-B-32} variant.

\textbf{Whisper.}
OpenAI's Whisper~\cite{whisper} is an encoder--decoder speech model trained
on 680{,}000 hours of multilingual web audio. The small variant
(244M parameters, 250\,MB) supports 99 languages and is small enough to run
on CPU for short-query inference. We use Whisper-small as the speech front
end for our voice modality.

\textbf{FAISS.}
FAISS~\cite{faiss} provides efficient similarity search over dense vector
collections. For our gallery size (5{,}000 images), the flat inner-product
index (which is exact, not approximate) is small (10\,MB) and fast enough
that the two-stage retrieve-and-rerank pattern is unnecessary in practice;
we keep it for architectural consistency with larger deployments.

\textbf{Cross-lingual embedding limitations.}
A growing literature documents the fact that shared multilingual embeddings
trade off per-language fidelity. For LASER~\cite{laser} and
XLM-R~\cite{xlmr}, low-resource languages occupy compressed regions of the
shared space, leading to homograph collisions where surface forms shared
across languages compete for a single representation. Our Azerbaijani
finding is a specific, query-time observation of this phenomenon in mCLIP.

% ====================== 3. Architecture ======================
\section{System Architecture}\label{sec:arch}

The system consists of three layers (Figure~\ref{fig:arch}):
a React frontend, a FastAPI backend that loads the model and indexes once
at startup via the \texttt{lifespan} mechanism, and the underlying ML
library composed of the mCLIP wrapper, FAISS indexer, and Whisper handler.

