\subsection{Three input modalities}
\begin{description}[leftmargin=*]
\item[Text query.] The user types a natural-language query in any
  XLM-R-supported language. The backend tokenises the query with the
  XLM-R-Large tokenizer, runs it through the mCLIP text encoder
  (\texttt{XLM-RoBERTa-Large} + linear projection to 512-d), L2-normalises
  the result, and performs an inner-product search against the FAISS image
  index.
\item[Voice query.] The frontend records audio via the browser's
  \texttt{MediaRecorder} API and streams a WebM/Opus blob to the backend.
  The backend decodes via \texttt{soundfile}, resamples to 16\,kHz mono,
  and feeds the array to Whisper-small running on CPU. The transcript and
  the detected language are then routed through the same text-query
  pipeline.
\item[Image query.] The frontend uploads a JPEG/PNG/WebP file via a
  drag-and-drop zone. The backend opens it with PIL, preprocesses it with
  the CLIP image processor, runs it through the CLIP ViT-B/32 visual
  encoder, L2-normalises, and queries the FAISS index --- yielding the
  visually-nearest images in the gallery.
\end{description}

\subsection{Retrieval}
We use a two-stage retriever: stage one performs a top-$N=100$ FAISS
inner-product search, stage two re-ranks the candidates by exact cosine
similarity against the cached normalised embedding block. For flat indexes
the two stages return identical ordering; we keep the architecture in
place because production deployments would substitute an IVF/HNSW index in
stage one, for which exact re-ranking is essential.

% ====================== 4. Methodology ======================
\section{Fine-Tuning Methodology}\label{sec:training}

\subsection{Data}
We start from MS-COCO val2017~\cite{coco}, which contains 5{,}000 images
each with five human-written English captions (25{,}014 captions total).
We translate every English caption into Arabic (\textit{ar}), Chinese
(\textit{zh}), French (\textit{fr}), German (\textit{de}), and Russian
(\textit{ru}) using the appropriate Helsinki-NLP \texttt{opus-mt-en-X}
model~\cite{opusmt}, producing 150{,}084 multilingual captions.
The split is performed at the \emph{image-id level} (4{,}000 train / 500
val / 500 test images) so that all five captions of any given image stay
in the same split --- this is critical: caption-level splits leak.

\subsection{Loss function}
The training loss is the sum of a symmetric InfoNCE term and a
hard-negative margin term. For a batch with $N$ image-text pairs let
$I \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times 512}$ and $T \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times 512}$
be the L2-normalised image and text embeddings. With temperature $\tau$,
the InfoNCE loss is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{InfoNCE}}(I, T) = -\frac{1}{2N} \sum_{i=1}^{N}
\Bigl[
\log \frac{\exp(I_i T_i^\top / \tau)}
          {\sum_{j} \exp(I_i T_j^\top / \tau)}
+
\log \frac{\exp(T_i I_i^\top / \tau)}
          {\sum_{j} \exp(T_i I_j^\top / \tau)}
\Bigr].
\end{equation}
We additionally select the $k$ hardest in-batch negatives per anchor (those
with the highest similarity score that are not the positive) and add a
margin term that penalises confusion with these specifically. The
temperature is fixed at $\tau = 0.07$ and $k = 1$ hard negative per sample.

\subsection{Architecture and capacity}
The full mCLIP model has 561M parameters. We freeze the entire CLIP ViT
visual encoder ($\approx$88M parameters) and the bottom 16 of the 24
XLM-RoBERTa-Large transformer layers, leaving \textbf{110M trainable
parameters} concentrated in the top 8 text-encoder layers plus the
512-dimensional projection. Gradient checkpointing on the text encoder
reduces peak VRAM at the cost of extra compute, allowing the model to fit
in the 6\,GB VRAM budget of an RTX 3060 Laptop GPU.

\subsection{Optimization}
We train with AdamW at learning rate $10^{-5}$, weight decay $0.01$, linear
warmup of 300 steps, batch size 8 with 16-step gradient accumulation
(effective batch 128), mixed-precision (\texttt{torch.cuda.amp}), and
gradient clipping at $\|g\| \leq 1.0$. Early stopping monitors validation
Recall@1 with patience 4. Maximum epochs is 15 but we observe convergence
much earlier.

% ====================== 5. Implementation ======================
\section{Implementation}\label{sec:impl}

\subsection{Backend}
The backend is a FastAPI application (Python 3.12) that loads all heavy
state once at server startup via the \texttt{lifespan} context manager and
exposes four routes:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python, caption={Backend routes (excerpt).}]
@router.post("/search")
def search(req: SearchRequest, request: Request):
    retriever = state.two_stage if req.retriever == "two_stage" \
                                else state.text_bridge
    results = retriever.retrieve(req.query, top_k=req.top_k)
    return SearchResponse(query=req.query, results=hits, latency_ms=...)

@router.post("/search/voice")
async def search_voice(audio: UploadFile, top_k: int = Form(5),
                       language: str | None = Form(None)):
    audio_bytes = await audio.read()
    info = state.voice.transcribe_bytes(audio_bytes, language=language)
    results = state.two_stage.retrieve(info["text"], top_k=top_k)
    return VoiceSearchResponse(transcript=info["text"], ...)

@router.post("/search/image")
async def search_image(image: UploadFile, top_k: int = Form(5)):
    pil = PILImage.open(io.BytesIO(await image.read())).convert("RGB")
    emb = state.model.encode_image(preprocess(pil))[0].cpu().numpy()
    candidates = state.image_indexer.search(emb / norm, top_k)
    return SearchResponse(query="<image>", results=hits, ...)
\end{lstlisting}

The image gallery is served as static files from \texttt{/images/}
(mapped to \texttt{data/images/}); the React frontend builds image URLs
from the COCO image ID, padded to twelve digits.

\subsection{Frontend}
The frontend is a Vite-bundled React 18 + TypeScript single-page
application. Three tabs --- text, voice, image --- share a common
\texttt{ResultsGallery} component that renders cards with rank/score
badges and opens an image-lightbox modal on click.

The voice tab uses the browser's \texttt{MediaRecorder} API to capture
audio. While recording is active, a Web Audio \texttt{AnalyserNode} drives
a canvas-based live spectrum visualiser, giving the user immediate
feedback that the microphone is being captured.

The text and voice tabs implement a heuristic warning function
\texttt{isProbablyShortAze} that flags 1--2 token queries containing
Azerbaijani-specific characters or known AZ single-token surface forms
(see \S\ref{sec:homograph}).

\subsection{Reproducibility}
All hyperparameters live in \texttt{config/config.yaml}. Caches
(HuggingFace, pip, torch) are redirected to the project root via env
variables set in \texttt{src/utils.py::setup\_caches}. The complete
pipeline is reproducible via the eight numbered scripts in
\texttt{scripts/}, executed in order. Tests covering the data split,
loss, metrics, retriever ordering, and FastAPI route schemas are in
\texttt{tests/}.

% ====================== 6. Experiments and Results ======================
\section{Experiments and Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Evaluation protocol}
We evaluate on the 500-image test split, which yields 15{,}012 test
queries (500 images $\times$ 5 captions $\times$ 6 languages, minus a
small number of empty captions). For each query, we compute Recall@1,
Recall@5, Recall@10, and mean Average Precision at 10 (mAP@10) by
treating the image whose caption was used as the single relevant item.
The same procedure is applied to both the pretrained mCLIP weights
(downloaded as-is from the M-CLIP HuggingFace repository) and our
fine-tuned weights (epoch-1 \texttt{best.pt}, val\_R@1 = 0.586).

