\subsection{Observations}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
  \item \textbf{Overall lift is essentially zero.} Fine-tuning produces a
    $-0.04$\,pp R@1 change, a $+0.72$\,pp R@5 change, and a $+0.07$\,pp
    mAP@10 change. None of these is operationally meaningful at the 5\,K
    scale.
  \item \textbf{Languages do not move together.} Chinese gains
    $+0.40$\,pp R@1, Russian regresses by $-1.04$\,pp R@1, and
    en/de/fr are essentially flat. R@5 improves for the historically
    weakest language, Arabic ($+1.80$\,pp), where R@1 barely moves.
  \item \textbf{Cross-lingual gap is preserved.} English leads, then
    French/German, then Russian/Chinese, with Arabic trailing by 5--7\,pp
    at R@1. The fine-tune does not collapse the gap; it preserves the
    pretrained ordering (Figure~\ref{fig:gaingap}b).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Comparison with the 1\,K data baseline (v2)}
A prior experiment of ours fine-tuned the same mCLIP architecture with the
same hyperparameters on a 1{,}000-image subset (referred to as \emph{v2}),
