% ====================== 7. Discussion ======================
\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Why more data did not help}
\label{sec:plateau}
The naive hypothesis would be that more training data is monotonically
better. The data disproves this: 5$\times$ more captions per language
produced a smaller fine-tuning lift than the 1\,K baseline. We propose
three related, non-exclusive explanations.

\textbf{(a) Trainable capacity bottleneck.} We freeze the visual encoder
and the bottom 16 of 24 XLM-R layers. The 110M trainable parameters
concentrate in the top 8 text-encoder layers and the 512-d projection
matrix. With 5\,K images, the InfoNCE loss reaches a regime where it
exhausts this capacity in one epoch and any further training optimises a
direction that does not generalise to held-out queries. The training log
shows monotonically decreasing val\_loss across epochs 1--4 while val\_R@1
monotonically declines, a signature of metric/loss disagreement that
typically indicates over-specialisation rather than under-fitting.

\textbf{(b) Saturation of pretrained alignment.} mCLIP's pretrained
alignment between XLM-R text and CLIP visual embeddings is already strong
on COCO-style data. The fine-tune is therefore not establishing new
multilingual alignment from scratch; it is only re-fitting the
512-dimensional shared subspace to the specific caption phrasings in our
training set. On 5\,K examples this becomes a corpus-fitting task rather
than a knowledge-acquisition task.

\textbf{(c) MT noise floor.} All non-English captions are
machine-translated from English by \texttt{opus-mt-en-X}. The captions
share lexical patterns characteristic of MT (literal renderings, limited
collocations, occasional malformed output). The model can fit these
patterns but the gain does not transfer to the (also-MT-generated) test
captions in any way that distinguishes from the pretrained baseline.

\subsection{Cross-lingual homograph collision in Azerbaijani}
\label{sec:homograph}
During manual demo testing in Azerbaijani --- a language outside our
fine-tuning set but included in XLM-R's pretrained languages --- we
observed a reproducible failure of the retrieval system at very short
queries. The Azerbaijani word \emph{it} means \emph{``dog''}; the same
surface form \texttt{it} in English is a pronoun with weak, abstract
embedding signal.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
  \item Query \texttt{``it tullanır''} (``the dog is jumping''):
    returns dog images. \emph{Correct.}
  \item Query \texttt{``parkda it''} (``a dog in the park''):
    returns dog images. \emph{Correct.}
  \item Query \texttt{``it''} (single token): returns visually unrelated
    images. \emph{Incorrect.}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Mechanism.} When the query contains additional Azerbaijani-specific
tokens (\textit{tullanır}, \textit{parkda}), the language context allows
XLM-R to resolve \texttt{it} as the Azerbaijani noun. With the single
token, no context is available, and the model's embedding is dominated by
the English-pronoun sense (English being the highest-resource language in
mCLIP's training distribution). The downstream visual retrieval then
returns whatever images happen to align with the abstract pronoun
embedding.

\textbf{Fine-tuning cannot fix this.} Our fine-tuning corpus contains no
Azerbaijani captions, so the AZ$\to$shared-space alignment is inherited
verbatim from XLM-R pretraining. Contrastive fine-tuning on the other six
languages does not reorganise the embedding for tokens it never sees.

\textbf{Mitigation in deployment.} We add a frontend heuristic
\texttt{isProbablyShortAze} that flags queries of 1--2 tokens containing
Azerbaijani-specific orthographic characters
(\textit{ə, ş, ğ, ç, ı, İ, Ü, ü, Ö, ö}) or surface forms in a curated list
of known low-frequency single-token AZ words (\textit{it}, \textit{ev},
\textit{at}, \textit{su}, \textit{yol}, \dots). When the heuristic fires
the UI displays an inline warning suggesting that the user lengthen the
query. The voice tab fires three variants of the same warning at different
points in the user's flow: pre-search (when the user explicitly picks
Azerbaijani in the language dropdown), post-detection (when Whisper
auto-detects an out-of-set language), and post-transcription (when the
returned transcript itself is short and probably Azerbaijani).

\textbf{Why this matters for the project.}
Cross-lingual homograph collisions are a known weakness of shared
multilingual embeddings, but most published benchmarks use full-sentence
queries that mask the issue. Retrieval at query time exposes it cleanly,
because retrieval queries are typically short. Our project provides a
specific, reproducible instance and a working UX-level mitigation.

\subsection{Limitations}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
\item The visual encoder is frozen; all fine-tuning gain depends on
  text-side adaptation. Unfreezing the visual encoder, even partially,
  is likely the highest-leverage next step (cf.\ \S\ref{sec:future}).
\item Multilingual captions are MT-generated from English. As a
  consequence, the text-bridge retriever (which matches queries directly
  against the caption index) achieves trivially high Recall@1 because the
  EN gallery contains exact source strings of the AR/ZH/FR/DE/RU
  translations. The two-stage retriever, which matches against image
  embeddings, is unaffected; all numbers in this report are two-stage.
\item Whisper-small's transcription accuracy on Azerbaijani is moderate
  at best. A larger Whisper variant (medium or large) would improve voice
  results on low-resource languages but at the cost of CPU latency or
  additional VRAM consumption.
\item The gallery is 5{,}000 images, far smaller than production
  galleries. Latency scaling, approximate-NN indexes, and engineering for
  100M-scale retrieval are out of scope.
\end{itemize}

% ====================== 8. Future Work ======================
\section{Future Work}\label{sec:future}

\textbf{Unfreezing the visual encoder.} The most likely path to a
meaningful R@1 lift is to thaw the bottom few layers of the CLIP ViT,
trading VRAM headroom for visual-side adaptation. A staged
freeze-then-thaw schedule should be tested.

\textbf{Including Azerbaijani in fine-tuning.} The homograph collision
documented in \S\ref{sec:homograph} is partially correctable by including
Azerbaijani captions in the fine-tuning set, even if those captions are
themselves machine-translated. This would force the AZ$\to$shared-space
alignment to be optimised rather than inherited verbatim from pretraining.

\textbf{Replacing MT-generated captions with human translations on a
subset.} A small held-out human-translated test set would let us
disentangle the model's quality from MT artefacts in the test queries
themselves.

\textbf{Attention-based explanation overlay.} Showing the user which
image patches contributed most to the retrieval score would both improve
trust in the system and expose interesting per-language attention
patterns.

\textbf{Larger Whisper variant on demand.} The voice tab could
optionally switch to \texttt{whisper-medium} when the user selects a
low-resource language, accepting higher CPU latency for better
transcription accuracy.

% ====================== 9. Conclusion ======================
\section{Conclusion}

We presented a multilingual text-to-image retrieval system that combines a
fine-tuned mCLIP backbone, an OpenAI Whisper-small voice front end, and a
FAISS-based retrieval index, exposed through a FastAPI backend and a React
frontend. The system supports three input modalities --- text, voice,
image --- against a shared 512-dimensional embedding space and a
5{,}000-image gallery.

Our controlled comparison of pretrained vs.\ fine-tuned weights across six
languages produces a null result on overall Recall@1, accompanied by a
small positive shift on Recall@5 and mAP@10. We propose a
trainable-capacity bottleneck hypothesis to explain the absence of a
5$\times$-data lift and support it with the training trajectory. Beyond
the quantitative findings, we documented a specific cross-lingual
homograph collision encountered in Azerbaijani queries, identified its
mechanism in mCLIP's shared embedding space, and shipped a deployable
UX-level mitigation in the frontend.

The codebase, the reproducible scripts, the evaluation reports, and the
frontend are released at
\href{https://github.com/Bayrii/text2image-multilingual-retrieval}
{github.com/Bayrii/text2image-multilingual-retrieval}.

